<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shays' Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1786–1787</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shays' Rebellion was an armed uprising in western Massachusetts in 1786–1787 by indebted farmers, led by the war veteran Daniel Shays, against high taxes and the seizure of property for debt. Its suppression by a state-funded militia exposed the weakness of the national government under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/articles-of-confederation">Articles of Confederation</a> and strengthened support for the Constitutional Convention of 1787.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shays-rebellion.jpg" alt="Shays' Rebellion" width="320" height="258" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C. Kendrick · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shays_forces_flee_Continental_troops,_Springfield.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
